## 5. Handling Core Updates

### Update Strategy

```bash
# Check for core updates
pip list --outdated | grep django-core
pnpm outdated | grep @django-core

# Update to latest patch (safe)
pip install --upgrade django-core-auth~=1.0.0
pnpm update @django-core/design-system --latest

# Update to latest minor (review changelog)
pip install --upgrade django-core-auth~=1.1.0
pnpm update @django-core/* --latest

# Test after updates
pytest
pnpm test
```

### Migration Handling

```bash
# Core modules may add migrations
python manage.py showmigrations | grep django_core

# Run core migrations
python manage.py migrate django_core_accounts
python manage.py migrate django_core_organisations

# Then your migrations
python manage.py migrate
```

### Breaking Changes

Core follows semantic versioning:
- **Patch (1.0.x)**: Bug fixes, safe to update
- **Minor (1.x.0)**: New features, backward compatible
- **Major (x.0.0)**: Breaking changes, requires migration

**When major update released**:
1. Read `CHANGELOG.md` in core repo
2. Check deprecation warnings in your logs
3. Update imports/API calls per migration guide
4. Test thoroughly before production deploy

